From brian_cade at usgs.gov  Wed Jul  2 20:51:47 2003
From: brian_cade at usgs.gov (Brian S Cade)
Date: Wed, 02 Jul 2003 14:51:47 -0600
Subject: Re: GAM vs spatial analysis vs a combination
Message-ID: <OFA366BC64.D22E840C-ON87256D57.0070385D@cr.usgs.gov>

One possibility if there is a fairly good size sample (n > 100) with good
coverage of the spatial extent is to fit a spatial trend surface by cubic
polynomials of latitude and longitude coordinates in a linear model and
then add in the relevant environmental covariate (depth).  You can of
course do some model selection to pick the most parsimonious reduced
parameter form of the full cubic polynomial.  The environmental covariates
(e.g., depth) can be included in a simple additive fashion, or perhaps
after some nonlinear transformation.   You can test whether the spatial
trend coefficients are significant after having included the environmental
variable (depth) which will itself be spatially structured.  After
selecting the best model for the cubic polynomial spatial trend surface and
the environmental covariates, you could model the small scale spatial
autocorrelation in the residuals to improve the covariance estimates.   See
for example, Legendre et al. 1997.  Spatial structure of bivalves in a
sandflat: Scale and generating processes.  J. Experimental Marine Biology
and Ecology 216:99-128).  My experience with most ecological data of this
type is that the larger scale spatial trend modeled with the cubic
polynomial removes most of the significant spatial autocorrelation, and
that the remaining small scale spatial autocorrelation in the residuals is
rather trivial.  Often it is statistically satisfying to adjust for it, but
doing so rarely makes substantial difference in inferences (hypothesis
tests or confidence intervals), although conceivably it could.  I would
guess that you could follow some similar protocol with a GAM, but
hypothesis testing and interpretation may not be as straight forward.  The
key issue for either approach would be that some of the effect of the
environmental variable (depth) is likely to be confounded with the effect
of the spatial trend and this is difficult (impossible) to completely
disentangle.

