A biologist would like to predict biovolume of aquatic plants at points
in lakes given data on biovolume, depth, latitude and longitude. Could
someone suggest good references on how a covariate such as depth or
elevation might be added to a spatial analysis? [Have we missed
something in a spatial library or module?]

His initial analysis considered only latitude and longitude as
predictors, although depth has a strong influence on biovolume. It was
done in Arc-view by inverse distance weighting, spline interpolation,
and kriging.

One idea to incorporate depth in the analysis is by a GAM
fit1 <- gam(biovolume ~ s(depth, 8) + lo(lat, long, span=0.2))
based roughly on an example in Bowman and Azzalini (1992, p 156). But
is there an objective way to make the choice of span for the lo() term
(or the choice of df and span jointly)?

Another idea is to subtract the depth trend (fit by GAM with GCV to
guide choice of df), then try kriging the residuals to describe local
deviations from the overall depth effect, then add the GAM prediction
and the kriging prediction to get a combined biovolume prediction. Is
this the best way to go?

