From spencer.graves at PDF.COM  Thu Jul  3 14:51:26 2003
From: spencer.graves at PDF.COM (Spencer Graves)
Date: Thu, 03 Jul 2003 07:51:26 -0700
Subject: Re: power of a matrix
References: <200307031322.h63DMO0F011702@fisher.utstat.toronto.edu>
Message-ID: <3F0442EE.7090502@pdf.com>

	  Yes, Jo's suggestion is elementwise power, not matrix power. 
Hesterberg's solution only works for integer power.

	  Just playing:  The Cayley-Hamilton theorem applies to an arbitrary 
analytic function of an arbitrary square matrix, except with repeated 
roots required off-diagonal elements in the Jordon canonical form.  The 
following modification of my earlier solution permits the use of an 
arbitrary function with or without extra arguments.

matrix.function <-
function(x, FUN="^", ...){
# FUN(x, y) where x is a matrix, y a scalar
# matrix.function(x, e2=2) for x^2
        eig <- eigen(x)
        eigFUN <- do.call(FUN, list(eig$values, ...))
        x.y <- t(solve(t(eig$vectors), t(eig$vectors) * eigFUN))
        dimnames(x.y) <- dimnames(x)
        x.y
}

It still works for exponentiation, but you need to specify the power as 
an argument named e2:

> matrix.function(x, e2 = 2)
   c  d
a  7 15
b 10 22
> x %*% x
   c  d
a  7 15
b 10 22

Recall that exp(1i*x) = cos(x) + 1i*sin(x):
> matrix.function((1i) * x, "exp")
                      c                     d
a  0.8554232-0.4655815i -0.1663146-0.2226367i
b -0.1108764-0.1484245i  0.6891086-0.6882182i
> matrix.function(x, "cos") + (1i) * matrix.function(x, "sin")
                      c                     d
a  0.8554232-0.4655815i -0.1663146-0.2226367i
b -0.1108764-0.1484245i  0.6891086-0.6882182i

I'll leave it to someone else to check for repeated roots and if found 
to determine if all.equal(x, matrix.function(x, e2=1));  if this latter 
is false, then an appropriate error message should be printed.

Enjoy.
Spencer Graves

