From FowlerM at mar.dfo-mpo.gc.ca  Fri Jul  4 16:18:19 2003
From: FowlerM at mar.dfo-mpo.gc.ca (Fowler, Mark)
Date: Fri, 04 Jul 2003 13:18:19 -0300
Subject: Summary:using magnitudes of F in ANOVA to denote relevance
Message-ID: <1A4AC4BAB9C50A42854582B69B08C03402354E65@MSGMARBIO05>

Summary to the List on Comparing Magnitudes of F's

Thanks to all who replied. The discussion is reproduced in chronological
order, with some overview perspective at the end. I believe the question has
been answered.

Original Question

I had presented the following ANOVA to depict the potential relevance of
interactions, where we are seeking to determine if main effects alone could
be used to represent the essentials of the model with respect to predictions
of values for yland (annual time series):

                Df      Deviance        Resid. Df       Resid. Dev      F
Value   Pr(F)
NULL            NA      NA              4519            5135.242        NA
NA
cfv             34      517.41791       4485            4617.824
18.68637        0.000000e+000
yland           15      348.6338        4470            4269.191
28.539124       0.000000e+000
mland           9       164.50611       4461            4104.684
22.444086       0.000000e+000
AREA            5       105.90937       4456            3998.775
26.009187       0.000000e+000
yland:mland     117     279.9389        4339            3718.836
2.937919        0.000000e+000
yland:AREA      67      182.53822       4272            3536.298
3.345349        0.000000e+000
mland:AREA      41      90.57283        4231            3445.725
2.712542        2.896125e-008

 I thought that the magnitude of the deviance associated with year:month and
year:area, coupled with significance of the F test, indicated a need to
interpret the interactions (i.e. they were potentially relevant in a
pragmatic sense). This assertion was countered with the argument that the
smaller F's associated with the interaction terms indicated that main
effects would be adequate to do the job. Examination of the interactions was
felt to support this argument, as predictions from the interaction model
were mostly variations in degree, not contradictory. However I'm concerned
that the compatability between main effects and interaction model
predictions may be circumstantial (just happened to be true for this
particular example), and that the interpretation of F magnitudes to reflect
relevance of a term might be inappropriate. Opinions?

Responses to Original Question

[from Manuela Huso]
IHO, the "smaller" F's is not an interpretable factor.  That is what the
p-value is doing for you.  With over 4000 denominator degrees of freedom,
the F for your interactions says your particular data configuration is
extremely unlikely if in fact no interaction exists.  It is my understanding
that in order to interpret main effects when significant interaction is
present, an F statsitic for the main effect of around 100 times that of the
interaction is required.  

However, again, with over 4000 df in the error term, you can easily get
extremely precise estimates that do not reflect biologically meaningful
differences.  Why don't you just plot your data and see what the relevance
of the interaction is in biological (or whatever context is relevant) terms?
You may be dealing with a statistically significan difference that is not
biologically significant.

Hope this helps, 

Manuela

[from Robert Balshaw]

Have you looked at the predictions themselves, perhaps through a series of
plots?   If over the range you are likely to see in your data the
interactions do not result in substantive changes in the 'accuracty' of your
model, you might be justified in leaving them out.

Just a thought.

Rob

-- Robert Balshaw, Ph.D.
-- Senior Biostatistician, Syreon Corp.
-- Phone: 604.676.5900x220; Fax: 604.676.5911

My followup to the original question (responding to Robert Balshaw)

        Yes, we did that. That was exactly the justification for removing
interaction terms (the interactions did not suggest troubling differences in
trends, just differences like greater and lesser slopes of trends in the
same direction). So having reviewed the interactions, I'm not worried about
opting for the main effects model in this instance. My question is more
general. The rationale for dropping consideration of the interactions was
not explicitly because the interactions did not appear to contradict the
main effects interpretation. The interactions were also perceived as minor
concerns because the magnitude of the F values associated with the
interactions were much smaller than those associated with the main effects.
It is this rationale that concerns me, given the likelihood of it being
applied in other situations if accepted in this case. In other words, the
next time interactions with smaller F's than main effects are encountered,
this reasoning could preclude bothering to look at the interactions. I'm
questioning the principle in general, not the consequences for this example.
The example just illustrates the context in which the decision was made.

Responses after followup

[from Robert Balshaw]
I think you're right to be cautious about the 'drop interactions with
smaller F values' strategy and I think your 'interpret the predicted values'
strategy is the correct one.

It's easy to imagine a situation where an interaction with a smallish F
value could correspond to an plot showing that for the upper level/range of
A, the high level of B produces the optimal response, but at the low end of
A, the opposite was true.  If determination of "optimal conditions" was the
goal, you'd definitely want to consider the interaction.  However, even in
this scenario, if the response is "practically similar" with and without the
interaction, there's not much need to include it.

As usual, I guess the answer depends on what the real question is.   I think
my clients are tired of hearing me say this.  ;)

Cheers,

Rob

-- Robert Balshaw, Ph.D.
-- Senior Biostatistician, Syreon Corp.
-- Phone: 604.676.5900x220; Fax: 604.676.5911

[from Naomi Altman]
I would not drop interactions based on the relative size of F-tests.  I 
look at the interaction plots, as suggested here.

Some statisticians would NEVER drop interactions, because there are 
inferential problems having to do with this type of "pre-test" modeling.

Naomi S. Altman                                814-865-3791 (voice)
Associate Professor
Dept. of Statistics                              814-863-7114 (fax)
Penn State University                         814-865-1348 (Statistics)
University Park, PA 16802-2111

Response to followup and Naomi

[from Spencer Graves]
          George Box (1999) has said that the proper role of statistics is
to 
catalyze the process of scientific learning.

          What one does with the interactions depends at least partly on 
whether one is doing exploratory or confirmatory analysis.  Even in a 
confirmatory investigation, I think that failing to also consider the 
data from an exploratory perspective is negligent and a huge waste of 
the cost.  In this regard, I like to think of the multiple levels of 
each main effect as arrayed on a continuum, with the continuum to be 
elucidated.  In this regard, the (g-1)x(h-1), etc., degrees of freedom 
can be decomposed into that many individual terms that correspond to a 
Taylor series expansion in terms of the latent variables behind each 
main effect.  This leads us to consider Tukey's one degree of freedom 
for non-additivity and generalizations by Mandel and others, discussed 
in Milliken and Johnson (1989).

hope this helps.  spencer graves

          Box, George E. P. (1999) "Statistics as a Catalyst to Learning by 
Scientific Method", Journal of Quality Technology, 31: 16-72;  reprinted 
in Tiao et al. (eds) Box in Quality and Discovery (NY:  Wiley 2000, pp. 
170-188).

          Milliken, G. A., and Johnson, D. E. (1989) Analysis of Messy Data,

vol. 2:  Nonreplicated Experiments (NY:  Chapman and Hall, pp. 7-40).

My overview

        Relative magnitudes of F between main effect and interaction terms
do not reflect the practical relevance of the interaction terms. If an
interaction term is significant, the interactions need to be examined to
determine their import.
        Some of the discussion suggests that the comparison of main effect
versus interaction F magnitudes has been seen elsewhere, but that the
differential would need to be very much greater than the example here. I
wonder if this might simply reflect the extremely low likelihood of
'import', given a situation where even total association of the interaction
with just one of the degrees of freedom in the term would still be
inconsequential.
        Tukey's 1 df test appeared to be suggested as having an application
to the problem. Unfamiliar as I am with this test, I have not figured out
how it might improve on the current method. It seems to be yet another way
of testing for significant interactions. I was not thinking that we had a
problem with the determination of significance by the F test. Perhaps the
Tukey and F tests differ in some essential fashion, such as weighting
contradictory slopes in interactions, that would matter here? [If the
Millikan and Johnson volume will clarify this, I should have it sorted out
soon enough]

Response to all above

[from Stephen Smith]

Mark:

I agree with the main thread of your respondents. I have never used relative
magnitudes of F statistics to decide upon whether or not terms should be
retained or removed in a model.  With respect to interactions, evaluating
with respect to interaction plots can be useful (not always) for
non-experimental design data such as we have (e.g., catch rates) for seeing
if all combinations have been observed or are even possible.  While
comparing predictions with and without interaction terms in the model is
also a good idea, you are conditioning on the observed combination of
factors and you have to make sure that most or all combinations have been
accounted for.  

Tukey's test was suggested by one of your respondents.  My experience with
this test is that it is used to test for additivity of model terms after a
transformation has been used.  I am not familiar with using it for
evaluating interactions.

Stephen

