problems. Thanks again!

Steve Friedman:

First thing I will suggest is that you do not do this analysis using GIS 
designed software. ARC-view is not a statistical software package - even though 
there are statistical operations in the system.  You must use a stats package 
such as Splus which has spatial statistical applications built directly into 
the systems (Splus Spatial Library).

Second I would suggest that you treat the depth as an other predictor of 
biovolume the same way you are using the lat and longitude coordinates.  Depth, 
I assume, is really a surrogate for light penetration and should be the really 
focus of the analysis, not the location data.

Rick Ditmars:

Cokriging may be what you are looking for, although the spatial stats module 
lacks that capability. Other geostat libraries may have it, though. I'm not 
sure about GeoS/GeoR, but they may be worth looking into.

Pete Meyer:

I heard a presentation by Leah Welty at Hopkins Biostat on some work she was 
doing as part of her dissertation at U of Chicago under Mike Stein, where they 
were trying to model the amount of phytoplankton in Lake Michigan.  It sounds 
like some of their work might be useful to you.  You can find her CV and 
references at:

http://galton.uchicago.edu/~welty/welty_cv.pdf 

Brian Cade:

One possibility if there is a fairly good size sample (n > 100) with good 
coverage of the spatial extent is to fit a spatial trend surface by cubic 
polynomials of latitude and longitude coordinates in a linear model and then 
add in the relevant environmental covariate (depth).  You can of course do some 
model selection to pick the most parsimonious reduced parameter form of the 
full cubic polynomial.  The environmental covariates (e.g., depth) can be 
included in a simple additive fashion, or perhaps after some nonlinear 
transformation.   You can test whether the spatial trend coefficients are 
significant after having included the environmental variable (depth) which will 
itself be spatially structured.  After selecting the best model for the cubic 
polynomial spatial trend surface and the environmental covariates, you could 
model the small scale spatial autocorrelation in the residuals to improve the 
covariance estimates.   See for example, Legendre et al. 1997.  Spatial 
structure of bivalves in a sandflat: Scale and generating processes.  J. 
Experimental Marine Biology and Ecology 216:99-128).  My experience with most 
ecological data of this type is that the larger scale spatial trend modeled 
with the cubic polynomial removes most of the significant spatial 
autocorrelation, and that the remaining small scale spatial autocorrelation in 
the residuals is rather trivial.  Often it is statistically satisfying to 
adjust for it, but doing so rarely makes substantial difference in inferences 
(hypothesis tests or confidence intervals), although conceivably it could.  I 
would guess that you could follow some similar protocol with a GAM, but 
hypothesis testing and interpretation may not be as straight forward.  The key 
issue for either approach would be that some of the effect of the environmental 
variable (depth) is likely to be confounded with the effect of the spatial 
trend and this is difficult (impossible) to completely disentangle.

Marc Vayssières:

I am not sure if it is applicable to your problem but you may want to look at 
co-kriging (see an example at 
http://www.gammadesign.com/Overview/Cokriging.html).
It is available in ArcGIS Geostatistical Analyst 
(http://www.esri.com/software/arcgis/arcgisxtensions/geostatistical/overview.html).

Henrik Aalborg Nielsen: 

[About the choice of span or df and span jointly] Try using R with the package 
mgcv for this (http://cran.us.r-project.org).  From the download-page:

"Routines for GAMs and other generalized ridge regression problems with  
multiple smoothing parameter selection by GCV or UBRE. Includes an  
implementation (not a clone) of gam()."

Naomi Altman:

Try the following paper:

1.      (1997) Hobert, J.P.,  Altman, N.S. and Schofield, C.L.  Spatial 
Analysis of  the Fish Species Richness of Adirondack Lakes:  Applications of 
Geostatistics and Nonparametric Regression. Journal of the American Statistical 
Association, 92, 846-854.

Lee Turner:

I think your last possibility sounds like the best one...'subtract the depth 
trend etc...'
You might look into median polish. I know that folks with lat/long data have 
used the technique to remove trend. Have a look at Tukey, J. W. (1977). 
Exploratory Data Analysis, Reading Massachusetts: Addison-Wesley. 
I don't remember the S+ code for median polish off the top of my head and I am 
heading out the door in about 10 minutes so I apologize for the brief nature of 
this message. 
I  would use kriging preferentially as it will be the best linear unbiased 
estimator in the case of these data. 

Original message from Charles Anderson below:

A biologist would like to predict biovolume of aquatic plants at points in 
lakes given data on biovolume, depth, latitude and longitude. Could someone 
suggest good references on how a covariate such as depth or elevation might be 
added to a spatial analysis? [Have we missed something in a spatial library or 
module?]

His initial analysis considered only latitude and longitude as predictors, 
although depth has a strong influence on biovolume. It was done in Arc-view by 
inverse distance weighting, spline interpolation, and kriging. 

One idea to incorporate depth in the analysis is by a GAM
fit1 <- gam(biovolume ~ s(depth, 8) + lo(lat, long, span=0.2)) 
based roughly on an example in Bowman and Azzalini (1992, p 156). But is there 
an objective way to make the choice of span for the lo() term (or the choice of 
df and span jointly)?

Another idea is to subtract the depth trend (fit by GAM with GCV to guide 
choice of df), then try kriging the residuals to describe local deviations from 
the overall depth effect, then add the GAM prediction and the kriging 
prediction to get a combined biovolume prediction. Is this the best way to go?

Thanks to all who reply. I will summarize for the group.

