Dear Luis

I am sure that Andy Liaw's suggestion (below) is a good one.    However, one 
must bear in mind that the theorem underlying the quotation from Minnotte is 
just that, a theorem, so cannot be wrong.  The problem therefore arises from 
failure of the assumptions of the theorem, and I think it's mostly due to 
discretisation.   

(Incidentally problems in using this theorem also arise if you try to improve 
estimates by using say a local linear smoother with a Gaussian kernel.  For 
this case also examples arise where there are small spurious modes, apparently 
in conflict with the mode-preserving property of the Gaussian kernel.  Once one 
realises that the "equivalent kernel" (see e.g. book by Fan and Gijbels) in 
this case is not Gaussian, one sees where the problem is.  It seems that the 
theorem (due to Polya, I think) is quite delicate: it is specifically about 
_convolution_ with _Gaussian_ kernels.)

For a start, the Gaussian kernel implemented in SPlus's density function is 
only an approximation.  This bites in at least two ways here: the kernel is 
only approximate, and in particular has compact support.  If it is truncated 
(and, one assumes, re-weighted), there is a jump discontinuity at each end of 
the support, and this will show up in small bumps in the density estimate.  In 
turn these bumps can lead to spurious local maxima.  (They won't always.)  

In addition, there is of course the discretisation error in the test for a 
local max.  In your test for a local max, I tried changing the values 0.0000005 
to 0.0000001 and got different results.  I also plotted the density estimates 
over a range of values of the bandwidth and found that the mode-tree graph was 
not showing the visually obvious modes correctly, presumably because of the 
nature of your is.mode test.  Using the peaks function instead gives simpler 
code and easier to understand results, but there are still spurious 
discontinuities due, I think, to the first sort of discretisation error.  

For those interested I append my modification of your code.

Hope this helps

Alun
