From asanquer at virbac.fr  Thu Jul 10 07:17:21 2003
From: asanquer at virbac.fr
Date: Thu, 10 Jul 2003 09:17:21 +0200
Subject: ANOVA on repeated measures with missing data
Message-ID: <OF41295262.EF90FC8D-ONC1256D5F.002759F8-C1256D5F.0028342B@virbac.fr>

Dear All,

Thanks to James Roger, Renaud Lancelot, Michael Riggs, Gunter Bert, and
Bill Venables for their help. Hereafter the summary of their response.

James Roger:
With missing data the problem becomes much more complicated. The Time
effect
can now be estimated between subjects as well as within subjects. Hence
it's
entry into the upper stratum.

This is similar to the situation in incomplete block designs where terms
can
be estimated at both stratum levels.

One way of approaching missing data in repeated measures is to delete all
the data for any subject for whom some of the data is missing. Then
everything is estimated at a single strum level again. Whether you want to
do this is up to you and involves several questions, including a look at
why
the data is missing.

Michael Riggs:
You should consider using a mixed model to analyze your repeated measures.
As long as data our missing at random you can fit the model and you will
get
reasonable results. See the book "Mixed effects models in s and S-plus" by
J. Pinheiro and D. Bates. The material you are interested in is in chapter
5. The split-plot approach you are using will not give you good estimates
even when you have complete data; when you have missing data the estimates
are even worse.

Take a look at this material; you might find it more helpful:

http://www.ats.ucla.edu/stat/Splus/seminars/Repeated_Measures/repeated_measu

res.htm

Gunter Bert:
The missing values introduce imbalance and make the estimates
non-orthogonal, which means they can't be interpreted as you like.Consider
the "Time" term, for example. In your original design (no missings)the
difference in averages of ID's between any ID's does not involve any Time
term (assuming ID effect is additive, of course), so there is no
information
on Time in the analysis of ID averages. Hence the Time term appears only in
the Within portion of the analysis.  But with missing values, the
differences will almost certainly include Time terms that do not cancel (
unless the missingness occurs by luck in a perfectly balanced way). So the
Time effect can be estimated from both the Id averages AND the within group
treatment, ergo the anova that you received.

Bill Venables:
When there are no missing values I presume the design is then orthogonal,
in
which case you will have information on each separate effect in one stratum
only.  (This is the goal of most split-plot designs, which you have).

When there are missing values the design is no longer orthogonal and you
have information on some effects both from within and between Id
comparisons.  I would recommend you try lme() for such non-orthogonal
cases,
by the way, as it automatically recovers 'inter- and intra-block'
information and packages the result in possibly a less puzzling way.

