Russell.Ivory at MerrickBank.com wrote:
> I have two questions with using the T/F logic of arima.mle$"converged"
>
>
>
> (1)  It seems that S-Plus is reversing the logic in the following
> statements:
>
>
>
>  > arima.mle(Cendant1.dollars, model = list(order = c(5, 1, 0)))$converged
>
> [1] F
>
>  > result.temp <- arima.mle(Cendant1.dollars, model = list(order = c(5,
> 1, 0)))$converged
>
>  > result.temp
>
> [1] F
>
>  > if(result.temp = F) 100 else 5
>
> [1] 5
>
>
>
> If the model did not converge and the result is 'F', shouldn't the 'if
> statement' (which is true that result.temp = F) return 100 rather than 5?

Use == in if statements. Or if the variable is.logical then no 
comparison is needed. The `=' sign is also used for assignment in S-PLUS 6.

if(result.temp == F) 100 else 5

is equivalent to

if(!result.temp) 100 else 5

> (2) In trying to condense the code I get the following error:
>
>
>
>  > if(arima.mle(Cendant1.dollars, model = list(order = c(5, 1,
> 0)))$converged = F) 100 else 5
>
> Problem: couldn't find assignment function for "arima.mle"
>
> Use traceback() to see the call stack

Here `=' is being used as an assignment function as before, thus the error.

regards,
Sundar

