	  The "mle" method is preferred for almost anything, in my opinion. 
(The only thing better in general is informative Bayes, and that only if 
you have a legitimate prior.)  With "mle", one can test hypothesis by 
fitting nested models, e.g., with 2 vs. 3 factors, using 
2*log(likelihood ratio) is approximately chi-square with the number of 
degrees of freedom being the difference between the numbers of 
parameters estimated.  With "factanal", it may not be trivial to figure 
out how many parameters are actually estimated because of implied 
constraints that the factors be orthogonal and unit lenght or whatever. 
 I have not stayed current with this literature, but I would expect 
that most recent books on factor analysis would discuss this.  At 
minimum, I believe that the older books by Joereskog (or Joreskog) would:

@book{joer:soer:1979,
    Author = {Joereskog, Karl G. and Soerbom, Dag},
    Title = {Advances in Factor Analysis and Structural Equation Models},
    Year = 1979,
    Publisher = {Abt Books},
    Pages = {242}
}
@book{joer:klov:reym:1976,
    Author = {Joereskog, K. G. and Klovan, J. E. and Reyment, R. A.},
    Title = {Methods in Geomathematics 1: {G}eological Factor Analysis},
    Year = 1976,
    Publisher = {North-Holland/Elsevier},
    Pages = {221}
}

hope this helps.  spencer graves

