Spencer Graves wrote:

> See comments in line.
>
> Moshe Adrian wrote:
> > Got it.  One thing I am confused about :
> >
> > Let's say I am testing whether going from a 2 factor model to a
> > 3 factor model is justified statistically.
> > (thus, my degrees of freedom would be 1)
>
> The degrees of freedom will NOT generally be 1.  Suppose we have N
> observations on k variables.
>
>           * The 0 factor model estimates k uniquenesses.
>
>           * The 1 factor model estimates 1 commonality (or eigenvalue) plus
> (k-1) variables required to uniquely determine the corresponding factor,
> being a k-vector of length 1:  total (k+1+(k-1)) = 2*k.  Likelihood
> ratio (LR) test would have k degrees of freedom.
>
>           * The 2 factor model estimates 2 commonalities plus (k-1) variables
> for the first factor plus (k-2) for the second:  In addition to the
> restriction of length 1, the second factor must also be orthogonal to
> the first.  total = 3*k-1.  LR test of 2 vs. 1 factor has (k-1) degrees
> of freedom.
>
>           * The 3 factor model estimates 4*k-3.  LR test of 3 vs. 2 factors 
> has
> (k-2) degrees of freedom = 1 only if k = 3.  That 1 is for the
> eigenvalue, because the last factor / eigenvector is completely
> determined first (k-1).
>
> Please check any good book on factor analysis.  (Any book on factor
> analysis that does not include discuss this is not worth occupying shelf
> space except for to support research in history and sociology of science.)
>
> > Let's say that my output for (1-pchisq(2*d.lglk,d.df)), is .0395
> >
> > Then this tells me that going to a 3 factor model is justified, correct (if
> > I want a 95% confidence)
> > However going from a 3 factor model to a 4 factor model yields me
> > .2707 as the output (therefore, not justified).
> >
> > Am I interpreting these values correctly?
>
>           If you correct the degrees of freedom, then you have the standard
> usage, and that should produce reasonable results for you.  Realize,
> however, that your true error rate is probably greater than 0.05 because
> you are doing multiple tests.
>
>           There is a growing literature on alternatives, but I am not current
> with that literature.  The above should perform reasonably well for you
> in most applications.
>
